#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134806} :activity/id #uuid "fcf94a4b-8800-5fa9-a920-52e87eab8bba" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135101 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ be vectors in a vector space $V$ equipped with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle.$ \n\nTwo vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ are **orthogonal with respect to the inner product** $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$ if their inner product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x} , \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nIf the two vectors are orthogonal, then we denote this by $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}.$ For instance, the vectors \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^3$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x} , \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1y_1 + 2x_2y_2 + x_3y_3\n$$\n\nare orthogonal, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (1\\cdot (-1))+0\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2-2+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ in a vector space $V$ equipped with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle$ is orthogonal to any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ since $\\langle \\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{x} \\rangle = 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "77689cf4-cbcb-5c6f-bd2f-c407e888c4cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5089 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "66487d7c-591b-5eac-bee6-8268950dfb3f" :step/math-academy-id 31877 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133682 :question/id #uuid "a4eeb5c3-c1d5-5eb2-bb4e-03de915e0dab" :question/math-academy-id "e-8579"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-poin